#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "a316177e-e646-576c-bcab-d6cb0a450a03" :question/math-academy-id "e-300" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\dfrac{x^3-27}{x-3}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 3} \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} &= \\lim_{x\\to3} \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 3} \\dfrac {3x^2}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= 3(3)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= 27 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "95c88e72-86e2-5627-b121-beafc7986d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-301" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nso we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nBut that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ &=4. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a1c3de-68cb-5e07-a71b-41f9ac77c832" :question/math-academy-id "e-302" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty\n$$\n\nrespectively,\nso we have the case\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\n.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\[5pt] &= 0. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8e96079a-3933-5562-a4f5-ddd87ac5d05f" :question/math-academy-id "e-303" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4}}$." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\nso applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {1}{5} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cd9f306-d637-5ca3-8d1d-0c1d2aec3aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066546} {:answer/id #uuid "ade54fef-10b8-5fce-a63a-0a3210d034e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066547} {:answer/id #uuid "0c103329-e81d-5fa1-a41a-eb77cecfef28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066548} {:answer/id #uuid "26f9c8fb-12f0-5d9b-a1ff-c25a8359ec2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066549} {:answer/id #uuid "689f3567-e3f9-5229-8450-666fb40527d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cd9f306-d637-5ca3-8d1d-0c1d2aec3aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066546} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e8a725b-c7f8-5b3a-b6a2-1034bd04bc47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066545}] :question/id #uuid "05d2d2b4-376a-594e-a55c-c9c3393ae7ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-31596" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\frac{x\\cos x}{\\ln (x + 1)}$?"}]}
